1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,350
Rewind the universe.

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:05,450
Watch galaxies draw closer.

3
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:07,910
Stars disappear.

4
00:00:08,390 --> 00:00:10,730
Even atoms come apart.

5
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:12,310
Keep going…

6
00:00:12,810 --> 00:00:16,630
and our equations predict something
extraordinary.

7
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:19,090
Infinite density.

8
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:21,140
Infinite temperature.

9
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:23,300
A singularity.

10
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:26,430
But what if that singularity

11
00:00:26,910 --> 00:00:28,090
was never real?

12
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:32,060
What if the universe’s most famous
beginning…

13
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,480
is where our best theory stops working?

14
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:38,095
To find out, we need to separate

15
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:40,690
three things that often get mixed together:

16
00:00:41,290 --> 00:00:43,060
the universe we can observe,

17
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:45,570
the Big Bang we have evidence for,

18
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:48,485
and the beginning we are still trying

19
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:49,610
to understand.

20
00:00:50,110 --> 00:00:51,490
Start with the evidence.

21
00:00:52,230 --> 00:00:55,545
On the largest scales, distant galaxies
generally

22
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:58,500
grow farther apart as space expands.

23
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:01,975
Run that expansion backward, and the
universe

24
00:01:01,990 --> 00:01:03,830
was hotter and denser.

25
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:06,995
We can still detect a faint glow

26
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:09,570
released when the universe became
transparent,

27
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:12,025
about three hundred and eighty thousand
years

28
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,260
into its expansion.

29
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,705
Today, that ancient light reaches us as

30
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:17,730
microwaves.

31
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:19,500
It fills the sky.

32
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:22,045
We are surrounded by a cooled-down

33
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:24,440
afterglow of the early universe.

34
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:28,580
The amounts of hydrogen, helium, and
deuterium

35
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:31,975
also match calculations of an early, hot

36
00:01:31,990 --> 00:01:33,220
cosmic furnace.

37
00:01:34,030 --> 00:01:35,050
Different clues.

38
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:36,320
The same story.

39
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:40,405
Our observable universe evolved from an
extremely

40
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:43,365
hot, dense state roughly thirteen point
eight

41
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:44,760
billion years ago.

42
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:47,435
But none of those clues is a

43
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:49,370
photograph of a singularity.

44
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:52,305
So where did the famous point come

45
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:52,760
from?

46
00:01:53,770 --> 00:01:57,060
Einstein’s theory of gravity describes how
matter,

47
00:01:57,250 --> 00:01:57,970
energy,

48
00:01:58,210 --> 00:02:00,340
space, and time affect one another.

49
00:02:01,070 --> 00:02:03,980
Apply it to a simple expanding universe,

50
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:06,760
and then follow the mathematics backward.

51
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:08,880
Distances shrink.

52
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:10,640
Density rises.

53
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:13,630
In the standard classical extrapolation,

54
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,565
the calculation reaches a boundary where
density

55
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:18,350
and temperature diverge.

56
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:21,240
The description cannot continue normally.

57
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:24,645
That boundary is associated with the Big

58
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:25,910
Bang singularity.

59
00:02:26,590 --> 00:02:27,920
Here is the catch.

60
00:02:28,770 --> 00:02:30,835
Following an equation beyond the conditions

61
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:32,000
where it has been tested

62
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:34,575
does not guarantee that nature follows it

63
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:35,050
there.

64
00:02:35,770 --> 00:02:37,400
An infinity in the calculation

65
00:02:37,690 --> 00:02:39,450
is not a measured object.

66
00:02:39,810 --> 00:02:41,235
It may be a warning that our

67
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:43,030
description is incomplete.

68
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:46,345
So did we discover the universe’s first

69
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:46,940
moment…

70
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:50,145
or the limit of the theory describing

71
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:50,560
it?

72
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:52,240
Before we answer,

73
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:53,645
we need to fix the picture in

74
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:54,230
our heads.

75
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:57,265
Imagine a glowing dot floating in an

76
00:02:57,280 --> 00:02:58,690
enormous black room.

77
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:00,650
The dot explodes,

78
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:03,100
and its pieces become galaxies.

79
00:03:03,830 --> 00:03:05,470
It is a memorable image.

80
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:08,475
It also sneaks an entire room into

81
00:03:08,490 --> 00:03:10,850
existence before the universe begins.

82
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:13,020
Where did that room come from?

83
00:03:13,390 --> 00:03:16,245
The Big Bang model does not require

84
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:19,770
an explosion into pre-existing empty space.

85
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:24,085
It describes an early hot, dense universe

86
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:25,780
and its subsequent expansion.

87
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:27,170
There is no

88
00:03:27,610 --> 00:03:30,455
established central launch site that
everything

89
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:31,770
is flying away from.

90
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:33,805
And the universe beyond what we can

91
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:36,105
observe might be much larger than our

92
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:37,040
visible region.

93
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:40,290
It might even be spatially infinite.

94
00:03:41,110 --> 00:03:43,945
An infinite universe can expand while
remaining

95
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:44,660
infinite.

96
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:48,195
Rewinding its expansion does not make all

97
00:03:48,210 --> 00:03:50,485
of it a tiny ball sitting somewhere

98
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:50,900
else.

99
00:03:51,770 --> 00:03:54,145
Everything we can see was once packed

100
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:56,330
into a much smaller region.

101
00:03:57,060 --> 00:03:58,985
That does not establish that everything
that

102
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,840
exists was once one little dot.

103
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:03,610
Now for the harder problem.

104
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:06,520
When we investigate atoms and particles,

105
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:08,350
we use quantum physics.

106
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:11,345
When we investigate gravity and the
large-scale

107
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:12,060
universe,

108
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:14,000
we use general relativity.

109
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:16,745
Both are astonishingly successful.

110
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:17,010
But

111
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:19,425
the earliest conditions push us toward a

112
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:22,725
regime where quantum effects and gravity
must

113
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:23,730
both matter.

114
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:26,195
We do not yet have an experimentally

115
00:04:26,210 --> 00:04:28,885
established theory that fully describes
that

116
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:29,530
regime.

117
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:31,360
To describe that moment,

118
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:33,075
we need more than a theory of

119
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:35,265
tiny particles or a theory of gravity

120
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:36,450
working separately.

121
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:38,710
We need them to work together.

122
00:04:39,310 --> 00:04:40,250
The singularity

123
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:42,675
may be where that missing physics becomes

124
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:43,470
unavoidable.

125
00:04:44,180 --> 00:04:45,550
Which brings us to the question

126
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:47,490
everyone wants to ask.

127
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:50,810
What happened before the Big Bang?

128
00:04:51,210 --> 00:04:52,720
There are several possibilities.

129
00:04:53,070 --> 00:04:55,165
None is an established account of our

130
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:56,170
ultimate origin.

131
00:04:57,010 --> 00:04:58,020
One possibility

132
00:04:58,330 --> 00:05:00,050
is that time has a beginning.

133
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:02,725
We usually imagine time as a road

134
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:04,750
stretching endlessly in both directions.

135
00:05:05,130 --> 00:05:07,140
But that picture is an assumption.

136
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:08,900
In some proposals,

137
00:05:09,130 --> 00:05:11,145
there is no earlier moment beyond the

138
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:12,770
universe’s initial boundary.

139
00:05:13,490 --> 00:05:15,380
Asking what happened before it

140
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:17,935
would be asking for a location outside

141
00:05:17,950 --> 00:05:19,050
the map’s domain.

142
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:21,480
That does not prove time began.

143
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:24,055
It shows why the word “before” might

144
00:05:24,070 --> 00:05:25,290
stop making sense.

145
00:05:25,910 --> 00:05:27,855
You cannot put an event earlier than

146
00:05:27,870 --> 00:05:28,940
the earliest moment…

147
00:05:29,450 --> 00:05:31,470
if an earliest moment exists.

148
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:34,535
Another possibility is that our expansion
followed

149
00:05:34,550 --> 00:05:35,970
an earlier phase.

150
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:39,325
Some speculative models replace a singular
beginning

151
00:05:39,340 --> 00:05:40,430
with a bounce.

152
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:43,960
A contracting universe reaches extreme
conditions,

153
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:47,210
and new physics allows expansion to follow.

154
00:05:47,730 --> 00:05:49,940
In that picture, our hot cosmic history

155
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:51,940
has a previous chapter.

156
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:54,775
But drawing a beautiful bounce is much

157
00:05:54,790 --> 00:05:57,430
easier than showing that one actually
happened.

158
00:05:57,820 --> 00:05:59,925
A convincing model must make physical sense

159
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:02,770
and survive comparison with observations.

160
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:05,325
We have not established that our universe

161
00:06:05,340 --> 00:06:05,950
bounced.

162
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:08,300
Then there is inflation: a

163
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:11,220
proposed period of extremely rapid
expansion

164
00:06:11,410 --> 00:06:13,190
very early in cosmic history.

165
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:16,155
Inflation can help explain several
large-scale

166
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:17,400
features we observe.

167
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:20,085
But what drove it, how it began,

168
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:21,995
and whether it happened in a particular

169
00:06:22,010 --> 00:06:24,220
form remain open questions.

170
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:27,455
It does not automatically explain why
anything

171
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:28,220
exists.

172
00:06:28,770 --> 00:06:31,125
Some inflationary scenarios suggest that
regions

173
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:32,820
beyond our observable universe

174
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:36,075
could develop different cosmic
histories—sometimes

175
00:06:36,090 --> 00:06:38,340
described as other universes.

176
00:06:39,070 --> 00:06:40,925
So did every universe come from the

177
00:06:40,940 --> 00:06:42,230
same singularity?

178
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:44,515
We do not know that other universes

179
00:06:44,530 --> 00:06:45,140
exist.

180
00:06:45,350 --> 00:06:47,565
We cannot establish a shared birthplace for

181
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:47,890
them.

182
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:50,455
The multiverse is a set of speculative

183
00:06:50,470 --> 00:06:53,705
possibilities, not an observed family tree
of

184
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:54,480
reality.

185
00:06:54,610 --> 00:06:56,255
And what about the idea that the

186
00:06:56,270 --> 00:06:58,200
universe appeared from nothing?

187
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:00,520
Be careful with that word.

188
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:02,600
In quantum physics,

189
00:07:02,910 --> 00:07:05,515
empty space is not necessarily the absence

190
00:07:05,530 --> 00:07:06,600
of everything.

191
00:07:07,370 --> 00:07:08,620
A quantum vacuum

192
00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:11,065
still belongs to a physical framework, with

193
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:13,350
fields, states, and rules.

194
00:07:14,290 --> 00:07:16,745
Explaining how something can emerge within
that

195
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:17,400
framework

196
00:07:17,690 --> 00:07:19,905
is different from explaining why the
framework

197
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:20,940
exists at all.

198
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:24,045
We should not hide the biggest unanswered

199
00:07:24,060 --> 00:07:26,410
question inside the word “nothing.”

200
00:07:27,710 --> 00:07:29,240
So what can we honestly say?

201
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:31,965
The hot Big Bang is supported by

202
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:32,660
evidence.

203
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:35,715
A physical singularity at the ultimate
beginning

204
00:07:35,730 --> 00:07:37,210
has not been established.

205
00:07:37,790 --> 00:07:40,710
What, if anything, preceded our early
expansion

206
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:42,200
remains unresolved.

207
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:44,840
Those are different levels of knowledge.

208
00:07:45,410 --> 00:07:46,965
And the gaps give us something to

209
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:47,650
investigate.

210
00:07:48,530 --> 00:07:51,310
Scientists can test early-universe ideas
through

211
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:53,975
increasingly precise measurements of
ancient

212
00:07:53,990 --> 00:07:54,500
light,

213
00:07:54,790 --> 00:07:56,490
the distribution of galaxies,

214
00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:59,935
and possible relic signals such as
primordial

215
00:07:59,950 --> 00:08:01,360
gravitational waves.

216
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:05,380
A theory earns confidence by explaining
evidence

217
00:08:05,590 --> 00:08:07,970
and making predictions that survive
testing.

218
00:08:08,970 --> 00:08:09,720
Meanwhile,

219
00:08:10,030 --> 00:08:11,945
you are already carrying part of this

220
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:12,570
history.

221
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:15,395
The hydrogen in your body traces back

222
00:08:15,410 --> 00:08:16,580
to the early universe.

223
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:18,955
Much of the carbon and oxygen needed

224
00:08:18,970 --> 00:08:20,620
for life was forged later,

225
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:22,950
through the lives and deaths of stars.

226
00:08:23,750 --> 00:08:26,255
You belong to a cosmic story whose

227
00:08:26,270 --> 00:08:28,560
early chapters we can reconstruct…

228
00:08:29,190 --> 00:08:30,695
even though we still do not know

229
00:08:30,710 --> 00:08:32,300
whether it had a first page.

230
00:08:32,610 --> 00:08:34,710
Rewind the universe again.

231
00:08:35,300 --> 00:08:36,630
Past the galaxies.

232
00:08:36,920 --> 00:08:38,340
Past the stars.

233
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:39,930
Past the atoms.

234
00:08:40,660 --> 00:08:42,650
At the edge of what we understand,

235
00:08:43,150 --> 00:08:45,155
do not imagine a tiny ball we

236
00:08:45,170 --> 00:08:46,390
have proved was there.

237
00:08:47,070 --> 00:08:49,165
Imagine a question we have not yet

238
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:50,510
learned how to answer.

239
00:08:51,010 --> 00:08:52,440
Did everything begin…

240
00:08:52,860 --> 00:08:54,845
or is a beginning simply where our

241
00:08:54,860 --> 00:08:56,450
explanation runs out?
